WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Film Courage: You said you've written&nbsp;
everything you possibly can for money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.770
I'm curious what's the difference between&nbsp;
writing for money and not writing for money?

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.360
Andy Guerdat, Television Writer, Producer,&nbsp;
Instructor, and Podcaster: Well, that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.840
great question. The difference between writing&nbsp;
for money and not writing for money is more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:20.560
just being a mercenary, I'm a working writer which&nbsp;
means I make a living or have made a living for 45&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.960
years writing. Other than teaching I haven't I&nbsp;
don't have a day job. I never have since I sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.600
my first script so I have to write what sells.&nbsp;
I have to write what people are paying money for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.000
so that means I'm often writing something that&nbsp;
isn't my first choice. I spent the last 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.560
to a large degree writing children's animation.&nbsp;
Well I wouldn't have chosen to write children's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.360
animation but that was what was paying so I&nbsp;
learned how to do it and I've enjoyed it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:55.200
I've done some very good work doing so that's one&nbsp;
distinction is I'm not all I write my own projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:03.320
separately not for money I'll write spec scripts&nbsp;
continue to do it always have sometimes they sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:09.920
sometimes they don't probably more often than not&nbsp;
spec scripts don't sell but that's me that's me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:18.920
putting my best work uh into the world but when&nbsp;
I write for money I'm trying to execute someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:25.840
else's vision as well as I possibly can that's the&nbsp;
one distinction but it's not quite as cold-blooded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.720
as all that because when you write for money that&nbsp;
means you're writing for a form that the producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.520
thinks will get that money back the money he's put&nbsp;
into it plus more plus a profit by definition that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.840
means you're writing for an audience there have&nbsp;
to be people out there who are going to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.800
this and that's great training because left to&nbsp;
their own devices all writers are going to tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.280
to get lost in their own head and just write&nbsp;
what they want to write and that's fine but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.000
they're often disconnect from the audience if you&nbsp;
haven't had the experience of watching an audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.600
see your work I've been lucky enough to have that&nbsp;
experience hundreds of times and it's been good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.680
for me to see what audiences like what audiences&nbsp;
don't like what they respond to what they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.760
respond to how they respond is great training and&nbsp;
I wish every writer could have that experience I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.720
have friends who are major screenwriters but they&nbsp;
may have only had four or five movies made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:31.120
their entire 20-30 years and even then very often&nbsp;
it wasn't really their movie somebody rewrote it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.520
or the director changed it and they and so they&nbsp;
have a limited amount of experience seeing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.600
audience respond to their work and what because I&nbsp;
have had that experience because I've written for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:50.720
money on TV shows or TV movies or movie movies&nbsp;
but because audiences responded to it and I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:58.680
in my job my paycheck depended on me satisfying&nbsp;
those audiences because of that I've been more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:06.080
in touch with the audience than I usually than&nbsp;
than most most screenwriters probably are I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.920
curious do you recall any interesting sort of&nbsp;
conversations where you've actually sat in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:19.840
heard positive and negative and how that changed&nbsp;
you or affected further storytelling positive or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.720
negative from the audience you mean right right&nbsp;
if you've been fly on the wall yeah for sure in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:31.920
terms of I did spent much of my career early in&nbsp;
my career it's about 20 years doing multi-camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.160
sitcoms and I don't know if that's a common term&nbsp;
everybody knows but multi-camera sitcoms are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.480
shot with four cameras on a sound stage like&nbsp;
Friends or Frasier, Everybody Loves Raymond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.720
that kind of thing and there's an audience behind&nbsp;
those cameras responding to the scenes as if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:57.760
were a play they're performed like a play scene&nbsp;
by scene well because of that 20 years 22 episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.000
of a season that's a lot of of episodes that I&nbsp;
either wrote or co-wrote or worked on seeing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.720
audience respond and so especially in comedy what&nbsp;
you're looking for is that verbal reaction a laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:17.200
if you get a laugh it's great if you don't it's&nbsp;
usually not good so in terms of that very often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.720
I'm constantly adjusting we might adjust between&nbsp;
takes very often when typical multi-camera sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.120
you do two takes usually sometimes three but&nbsp;
usually two takes of a scene and even in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.000
5-10 minutes between each take when the actors&nbsp;
are being touched up for their makeup and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.280
cameras are going back to position one the the&nbsp;
writers and producers are rushing in to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:46.560
and fix the joke or tweak this or make it work&nbsp;
better so it was a constant process and I also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:54.240
learned that laughter isn't the only way to read&nbsp;
an audience that I had a boss once who said all I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.800
want in that first scene I'd love to have a laugh&nbsp;
in the first scene but if I just get a gasp or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.600
moan or something that suggests they're engaged&nbsp;
in the story or gold that's what you're looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.640
for you're looking for are they engaged in the&nbsp;
story are they leaning in are they interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.320
in whatever the protagonist problem is and so it&nbsp;
could come in the form of a laugh but any kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:22.920
of response like that is more useful it's the most&nbsp;
useful thing any writer can have a dramatic writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.680
much more useful than a focus group for example&nbsp;
where focus groups of any kind are false there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:36.880
because you're asking people to put into verbal&nbsp;
responses what they felt and the average audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.920
member is not capable of doing that there's&nbsp;
no reason to expect them to be able to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:47.120
that it's hard for anybody to be able to say what&nbsp;
it was they felt half an hour ago when they watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:54.506
something they just can't do it and it's usually&nbsp;
not very useful watching them live is the best.

00:05:54.506 --> 00:05:59.480
Film Courage: Andy, you recently started a YouTube&nbsp;
channel and it's The Go Draft By Andy Guerdat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.880
you say the world doesn't need more&nbsp;
screenwriters it needs better movies?

